  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    A week ago I stood in the arrivals hall of Brisbane airport at eight in the morning, staring at a sliding door. Behind it: Lucy with a luggage trolley full of duffel bags, Joris pushing the stroller, Linnea in it, fast asleep. We hadn't seen each other for five weeks. Up to that moment, our emigration was a plan with open ends. Since then, it is our life.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anyone researching whether their family should move to Australia mostly finds polished travel reels and migration agencies that have never emigrated themselves. We want to do this differently. Here is the whole story: how two years in Byron Bay almost a decade ago slowly turned into a family, how a restaurant in Essen became a vegetable garden, how a cleared field in September 2025 triggered the decision, and what it was really like to give everything up, fly separately and come back together on the other side of the world. If you are weighing up whether your own family emigration project is realistic: this text is a comparison sheet, not an advert.
  </p>

  <div class="info-box">
    <p class="info-box__titel">Status &amp; note</p>
    <p>
      Status: <strong>5 August 2026</strong>, a good week after Lucy and the kids arrived. We keep updating this article quarterly, next when visa, housing and school start have become concrete.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>We are not migration advisers.</strong> What's written here is our experience. Authority facts are linked; binding information only comes directly from the <a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a> or a registered Migration Agent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- H2 1 (prologue, before the stages) -->
  <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    We are a family of four from Essen: Christian (37), Lucy (30), Joris (6) and Linnea (2). Since 28 July 2026 the four of us live in the Northern Rivers region on Australia's east coast. I am a trained chef and ran the restaurant die farm in Essen until the end of 2025. Lucy finished and passed her Master's thesis in interior architecture this summer, in the middle of the move. This story is ours, not migration advice, not a polished version, but what actually happened and keeps happening.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Joris</strong> starts school in Australia in early February 2027 and is already picking up English in play. What did he say when we told him we'd fly in three months? <em>"Why so late?"</em> That's his own voice. <strong>Linnea</strong> was too small to understand the scale, but she was right in the middle of it from the start, and that's a good thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. We are not migration advisers, we share what we are doing, not what you should do.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 1 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 1 · Essen → Byron Bay → Essen</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">2016–2018</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">where it all starts</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how-it-began">How it began</h2>
  <p>
    Lucy and I met in 2016, a few months before I left for Australia. In <strong>January 2017</strong> I flew to Byron Bay for my chef apprenticeship. Lucy followed in February, for a semester abroad she had organised at short notice. One semester became two. That one spontaneous gut decision is the origin of everything that follows: without Lucy's yes back then, we wouldn't exist as a couple, neither would Joris or Linnea, neither would Wildgewachsen nor this blog.
  </p>
  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil lang="en">
    Me flying ahead, Lucy following: in 2026 we repeated exactly what we had already done nine years earlier.
  </Randnotiz>
  <p>
    After Lucy's study time, we flew back to Germany together for a home visit. Lucy then stayed in Germany because she wanted to continue her studies. I went back to Byron Bay on my own to finish my apprenticeship. In total I lived there for almost two years; at the end of 2018, I returned to Germany for good.
  </p>
  <p>
    What no one had on their radar: the goodbye from family, friends, and especially from Lucy at the end of the home visit was hard. Not "a bit difficult". Hard. That fed into the decision to return at the end of 2018. We knew Australia as a place to live, we knew the region, we had built a daily life there. But in the end Germany was closer: family, language, a restaurant to take over.
  </p>
  <p>
    Who would have thought that a spontaneous yes to a semester abroad would become the line everything in our lives hangs on ten years later? Not us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. Departure January 2017, return at the end of 2018.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 2 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 2 · Essen, Rutherhof</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">2019–2025</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">seven years of die farm</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="return-restaurant">The return and the restaurant die farm</h2>
  <p>
    We returned to Germany at the end of 2018. The lease and business registration for the restaurant die farm in Essen followed on <strong>1 February 2019</strong>, the first renovation work we already started in December 2018. Seven years later, on <strong>31 December 2025</strong>, we closed the restaurant. Between those two dates lies the story from which our family, the plan to emigrate and the idea for this blog all grew.
  </p>
  <p>
    Homesickness was one factor: for family, for a language in which you can also discuss tax forms. The other factor was concrete: we could take over a restaurant in Essen. A one-off chance we didn't want to let slip.
  </p>
  <p>
    The restaurant sits on the Rutherhof in Essen, a complex with swing golf and football golf. We only ran the restaurant, not the whole complex. The concept was a countryside restaurant with ostrich meat as the speciality: steak, goulash, stroganoff, burgers. 60 seats inside, 60 outside. Through the games on the grounds, the restaurant was simultaneously an events venue: birthdays, company parties, weddings. One of those weddings was our own.
  </p>
  <p>
    In October 2025 we got married in our own restaurant, two months before closing. The cover image of this article is from that day: Christian, Lucy, Joris and Linnea, in the place where we worked for seven years. Shortly after, we locked the doors.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the last two years, something grew alongside it that no one had planned at the start: our own vegetable garden on the restaurant grounds. A piece of garden became proper market gardening, a hobby became a passion, and the passion became Wildgewachsen: our <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@die_farm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">YouTube channel</a> and now this blog too. Whoever follows us knows the garden more than the restaurant. That's no accident: the restaurant was the job, the garden was what we would have done even without pay.
  </p>
  <p>
    We had a good team that had worked together for years, and in hospitality that is not a given. We didn't stop because something had broken. We stopped because we wanted to move to Australia.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. Restaurant closed on 31 December 2025.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 3 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 3 · The field</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">September 2025</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">the moment</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="decision">The moment of decision</h2>
  <p>
    The honest answer to "when did you decide": <strong>early September 2025</strong>, and the trigger was a field.
  </p>
  <p>
    For years, Australia was a kind of pipe dream for us. A fondly remembered chapter of life and the recurring thought of experiencing it with the kids one day, but without serious ambition. Every time the idea came up, it failed at the same point: family and friends are here. It wasn't a dream we worked towards. It was a "someday" that never quite went away.
  </p>
  <p>
    In parallel, another question was running in the background, and it grew more pressing in the second half of 2025: how much longer do we want to sacrifice this much family time for the restaurant? Too much work, too little time with the kids, especially in the summer months when I was basically never home.
  </p>
  <p>
    In early September 2025 came the push that shoved both together: our <strong>second vegetable field</strong>, where we grew food for our family and for the restaurant, <strong>had to be cleared</strong>. Again. Again a lot of work that wasn't rewarded, again a plan that didn't work out. At exactly that point the restaurant doubts and the old Australia idea ran into each other, and "someday" became a joint decision: now or never. The decision to give up the restaurant and the decision to emigrate were made together. Not one after the other.
  </p>
  <a class="video-etikett" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=67OLkGf0_x4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    <span class="video-etikett__play" aria-hidden="true">▶</span>
    <span class="video-etikett__text">This moment exists as a video (in German): <strong>"Unser Feld ist weg. Schon wieder."</strong> on our YouTube channel</span>
  </a>
  <p>
    That the timing objectively made sense is something we only sorted out afterwards. Three things came together at once: Lucy was approaching her Master's. Joris was a year and a half away from school, exactly the window in which a school move abroad is easiest: he starts school over there instead of switching out of a German school halfway through. Linnea was small enough that she wouldn't cling to Germany. If not now, then not for another ten years, and I'd be 47 by then, Joris 16. My job, Lucy's degree, Joris' start at school: three upheavals, one window.
  </p>
  <p>
    We didn't decide logically. There was no spreadsheet in which "pro Australia" won. There were many evening conversations and, at one point, a shared clarity: if not now, then never. That clarity hasn't faded since, not even in the hardest weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. A gut decision with a very concrete trigger.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-australia">Why Australia – and why now</h2>
  <p>
    Australia was not a researched wish for us, we knew the region. Two years in Byron Bay are not a backpacking trip, they are lived daily life with bureaucracy, housing, laundry. "Why now" had three layers: the family one (Master's, school entry, Linnea's age), the professional one (I'd had enough of the restaurant, Lucy had a concrete direction with her interior architecture in a region full of interesting building projects) and the timing one (every extra year in Germany would have made the school switch harder for Joris).
  </p>
  <p>
    On where to live, we were realistic from the start: Byron Bay itself, the expensive inner-suburb wish, was not guaranteed with two kids and no fixed job. <strong>Mullumbimby</strong> was the favourite on paper, the criterion: 35 to 40 minutes between home and workplace, no further. As of today we actually live in exactly that area, in the hinterland near Murwillumbah, and are looking for our first proper home within exactly that radius. We never committed to one single town, we committed to this region.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we were looking for in Australia: more family time without shift culture. A climate you feel as a gift, not a burden. A work culture in which being a chef doesn't mean not seeing my own child for three weekends in a row in summer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: we know the region from two years in Byron Bay. The first place to live was an estimate, since overtaken by reality.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 4 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 4 · Dissolving everything</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">Oct 2025 – June 2026</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">lists, boxes, paperwork</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="preparation">How we prepared</h2>
  <p>
    Between the decision in September 2025 and my departure lay barely ten months. By early May 2026 we had ticked off most of the pre-bureaucracy steps: visa strategy (186 Employer Nomination Scheme), PTE Academic passed, Skill Assessment as a cook completed, migration agent engaged. Then came the entry visas, and after that everything moved faster than the mountain had felt beforehand.
  </p>
  <AufklappBox titel="The complete tick-off list in detail" lang="en">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visa strategy:</strong> the goal is the <a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">186 visa (Employer Nomination Scheme)</a>. That requires a sponsoring employer in Australia; we found one, as planned, on the ground.</li>
      <li><strong>PTE Academic:</strong> sat on 11 March 2026 in Hamburg with <a href="https://pearsonpte.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pearson PTE</a>, cost 270 €. Overall score 89, classified as Proficient (+10 EOI points). Clearly enough for 186 Direct Entry. Reading 66 and Writing 75 were below the Superior threshold (70/85); if we need +20 EOI points as a safety net, we'll retake the test in Australia.</li>
      <li><strong>Skill Assessment as a cook:</strong> completed through <a href="https://www.tradesrecognitionaustralia.gov.au/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trades Recognition Australia (TRA)</a>. Laborious and expensive, especially the documentation as a self-employed person. The item that cost us the most nerves.</li>
      <li><strong>Migration agent:</strong> we work with <a href="https://jkmigrate.com.au/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johannes Kunz of JK Migrate</a>, the most frequent recommendation in the German "Auswandern nach Australien" Facebook group. We had done little research of our own beforehand and were close to dropping the whole project over the visa complexity. The paid first consultation took the fear away: he explained the visa routes we didn't know and gave us a concrete step list. In hindsight a very good decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Restaurant closed</strong> on 31 December 2025.</li>
      <li><strong>Flat notice given</strong>, moving out end of July 2026, follow-up tenants found, taking over the kitchen.</li>
      <li><strong>eVisitor 651:</strong> issued on 28 May 2026 for all four of us, free, entirely online via my old ImmiAccount from my apprenticeship days. All four confirmations arrived within seconds by email. The eVisitor is valid for 12 months, with a maximum stay of 3 months per entry.</li>
      <li><strong>Flights booked:</strong> Christian 23 June 2026 (China Southern via Guangzhou, 467 €), Lucy + kids 26 July 2026 (Emirates via Dubai).</li>
    </ul>
  </AufklappBox>
  <p>
    One update belongs here because it costs money: when we wrote this article in May, the 186 application fees for the four of us were around 9,825 AUD. <strong>On 1 July 2026 they were raised: roughly 12,280 AUD</strong> for two adults and two children (main applicant 6,140 AUD). Whoever could lodge before the cut-off paid the old fee; ours wasn't ready yet. That's how honestly you have to budget: between planning and lodging, the fees can rise.
  </p>
  <p>
    The flat clear-out was the tougher part: wardrobes, kids' things, furniture. <strong>What didn't fit in the plane</strong> went into our crate system: numbered boxes, precisely documented, stored at Lucy's father's place, shipped to Australia on demand. The estimate: around 110 € per 10 kg box, around 170 € per 20 kg box (quotes we had, final price varies). A container service was not in the running for our volume; too expensive.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're interested in the <strong>full costs</strong>, what was paid, estimated and planned: everything is broken down in our cost article <a href="/en/cost-of-moving-to-australia-family-guide">Cost of Moving to Australia as a Family: Our Real Numbers</a>. The detailed <strong>186 visa route as a family</strong> is described here: <a href="/en/186-visa-australia-family-guide">186 Visa Australia: Our Family Route to the Employer Sponsored Visa</a>. And the <strong>Skill Assessment as a self-employed chef</strong> with all its documentation pitfalls is here: <a href="/en/tra-skill-assessment-chef-australia">TRA Skill Assessment as a Chef</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: every step done ourselves. Visa fees and conditions change all the time, binding information directly from the <a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="giving-up">What it means to give it all up</h2>
  <p>
    May 2026 was not relaxed. We were both stressed, many fronts at once, all roughly equally heavy. Lucy was writing her Master's thesis full-time, with a self-imposed deadline: 23 June, my departure. Officially the submission would have been mid-July, but realistically that wasn't going to work: as soon as I was gone, Lucy was alone with two kids and the rest of the flat. Her professor backed the pace, tight it was anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    During the day I researched between childcare, which often fell through, and renovation work in the flat; in the evenings I wrote the blog, edited videos, worked through housesitting portals and Facebook groups. And at some point the realisation set in that in Australia a huge amount would hang on me at once: without a job no sponsor, without a sponsor no visa, without a visa no bringing the family over. That chain was what kept me awake at night.
  </p>
  <aside class="nachtrag" aria-label="Note added at the end of June 2026">
    <p class="nachtrag__stempel">Added · end of June 2026</p>
    <p>
      On the evening of 29 June, Lucy uploaded what was finished and sat the examination. She
      passed: 2.0 in the second part, 1.8 overall in the German grading system. After a spring like
      this, a result she is genuinely happy with. We overcommitted with the
      double load, that is the honest assessment. But the item is ticked off, and the Master's
      degree isn't flying away from us anymore.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <p>
    Sorting out the flat, we had a clear division: I would have separated more radically, stored less. Lucy spent years lovingly sourcing the kids' things at flea markets; each piece a conscious choice. With the kids' things, I gave my opinion and left Lucy the decision. That was fair, because she chose those things back then and knows what she wants to keep as memories for the kids.
  </p>
  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" lang="en">
    "Cycling was part of our identity." Cargo bike, e-bike, kids' bike: all stayed in Germany. Batteries and size made shipping impossible.
  </Randnotiz>
  <p>
    There was one moment in those weeks that stuck with me: cycling with the kids, when it hit me that the bikes would stay behind. A cargo bike and e-bikes with their batteries are practically impossible to ship. We cycled almost daily. Small losses like that gave the departure more weight than any big paperwork.
  </p>
  <p>
    In that phase we talked more about the project and the planning than about ourselves. We knew it, and we held on, because it was a baseline with an end date. It was exhausting, sometimes sad, sometimes simply loud.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three days before my departure came an evening we hadn't planned this way: the wedding of one of my closest friends. It became, quite incidentally, the goodbye to my circle of friends and our last celebration together as a family in Germany. All four of us dressed up once more, warm evening light, and the suitcases already in our heads.
  </p>
