  <p>
    <em>For context: lived ourselves, the most exhausting phase of the whole project.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 5 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 5 · Essen → Guangzhou → Brisbane</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">23 June 2026</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">one flies ahead</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-flight">The goodbye and the flight</h2>
  <p>
    We said goodbye on 23 June at Essen central station, on a Tuesday morning, before Lucy took the kids to kindergarten and the childminder. We had deliberately agreed on this beforehand: as normal and unexcited as possible, so the kids would cope well. "We'll see each other in five weeks." Photos taken, one long hug, done. It worked. For Lucy and me it was hard anyway, we just didn't let it show.
  </p>
  <figure class="polaroid polaroid--rechts">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/de-abschied-selfie-kofferraum-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/de-abschied-selfie-kofferraum-p960.jpg"
        alt="Family selfie at the open car boot on departure day: Christian with a hat, Lucy, Joris with a cap and Linnea, all laughing into the camera"
        loading="lazy"
        width="960"
        height="1280"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="polaroid__caption">23 June, just before the goodbye: one last photo of the four of us for five weeks.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    One thing I would do differently today, and it definitely belongs in this article: I should have taken more time for the goodbyes. Especially the goodbye to my own parents was far too short in the stress of the final weeks, and that caught up with me in the first days in Australia more than anything else. If you are facing the same step: plan the goodbyes like an appointment. They matter more than the last box.
  </p>
  <p>
    The flight itself was the best of my four Australia flights so far: China Southern via Guangzhou, with a night in the airline's hotel, seven hours of sleep, a shower, a big bed. In Guangzhou I sent the kids one more video message before their evening and my second flight coincided:
  </p>
  <PullQuote quelle="Video message to Joris and Linnea, Guangzhou airport, 24 June 2026" lang="en">
    When you go to bed in a moment, I'll be on the plane. That means we can't call each other. But I still wanted to say good night once. And to say beep, beep, beep. We can talk tomorrow, when I have arrived. Take good care of Mum.
  </PullQuote>
  <figure class="beweis">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-flugkarte-frankfurt-guangzhou.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-flugkarte-frankfurt-guangzhou.jpg"
        alt="Seatback monitor showing the flight map Frankfurt to Guangzhou, route across eastern Europe"
        loading="lazy"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="beweis__caption">Evidence · seatback monitor, flight 1 of 2: Frankfurt → Guangzhou</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    On <strong>25 June at 8:30 am local time</strong> I landed in Brisbane, unusually quickly through immigration, by ten I was sitting in the rental car. "The suitcase is here, now out to the rental car. I am in Australia." That was all I managed to say into the camera in that moment.
  </p>
  <InlineVideo
    src="/assets/videos/au-landung-brisbane-loop.mp4"
    poster="/assets/images/au-landung-brisbane-loop-poster.jpg"
    caption="Approach into Brisbane, 25 June 2026, 8:30 am."
    lang="en"
    aspectRatio="16 / 9"
  />
  <p>
    The two-hour drive to Byron Bay I then cried through almost entirely. It was a highly emotional drive, the first time in months with room to think: "At first it was really sad, because there are so many things we simply won't experience for a very long time, or maybe forever." And then, from about Byron onwards, tears from the exact opposite: what we had worked towards for six months was coming true right there. The first thing I did at the beach: boots off.
  </p>
  <figure class="polaroid polaroid--links">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-barfuss-boots-byron-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-barfuss-boots-byron-p960.jpg"
        alt="Bare foot in the sand of Byron Bay right next to the just-removed leather boots"
        loading="lazy"
        width="720"
        height="1280"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="polaroid__caption">First stop, first official act: barefoot.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. The complete first week alone, with all its ups and downs, has its own article: <a href="/en/arrived-first-days-in-australia">Arrived in Australia: My First Days Alone</a>.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="five-weeks-alone">Five weeks alone in Byron Bay</h2>
  <p>
    The mission for the five weeks was clear: sponsor, car, accommodation. That was the order on paper; in reality everything ran at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Accommodation:</strong> the first nights a basic room found via Facebook, 300 AUD a week, not well kept, but a roof. From 2 July the first housesit: three weeks in a beautiful house with two dogs, hidden in the rainforest hinterland near Mullumbimby. Then an Airbnb in Byron Bay as a bridge, deliberately booked so something solid would be in place when the family arrived. Housesitting saved our start; from Germany we had collected nothing but rejections for months, on the ground that flipped.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Car:</strong> in week two we bought an old Jeep Grand Cherokee for <strong>4,500 AUD</strong>, our ceiling was 5,000. How buying a used car in New South Wales works in detail, with rego, stamp duty and all costs, is in its own article: <a href="/en/buying-a-car-in-australia-used-car-registration">Buying a Car in Australia</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sponsor:</strong> at the German expat meetup in Byron Bay, a few days after arriving, I was given the line that turned out to be completely right: walk around, be open, talk to people, hospitality is always hiring. That's exactly how it went. I walked into restaurants in person, introduced myself and was open about my visa status. In the end we found each other with <a href="https://www.threeways.store/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Threeways of the Valley</a>, a cafe-restaurant in the middle of the Byron Bay hinterland rainforest. We sat together over many evenings talking about plans and visions, and it fit on both sides. The team there is now working on their part of my 186 application: the nomination. Once it's through, I lodge my own application with Johannes' help.
  </p>
  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil lang="en">
    For the record: the sponsor did not come from a single application sent from Germany. It came from walking in.
  </Randnotiz>
  <p>
    And in between: the everyday life of a long-distance family. Joris and Linnea called every morning when they woke up, we had breakfast together with the phone propped up, in the evening Lucy called once more on her own. Meanwhile a heatwave in Germany kept the kids at grandma's pool while Lucy carried thesis, the remains of the flat and solo parenting all at once. That month was harder for her than for me, and that belongs here just as much.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. On the sponsor process: only a granted visa is binding; we describe the status, not a success that doesn't exist yet.</em>
  </p>

  <!-- STAGE 6 -->
  <div class="etappe">
    <span class="etappe__label">Stage 6 · Brisbane, arrivals hall</span>
    <span class="etappe__jahr">28 July 2026</span>
    <span class="etappe__notiz">four again</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="family-lands">The family lands</h2>
  <p>
    On 28 July, shortly after eight in the morning, the sliding door in Brisbane opened, 35 days after the goodbye at Essen central station. Joris pushed the stroller, in it Linnea, who simply slept through the big moment. Lucy behind them with a luggage trolley stacked with olive-green duffel bags: the whole life of four people, pressed into checked luggage. We hugged each other so tightly. What you say in a moment like that, I don't remember; it wasn't much.
  </p>
  <figure class="polaroid polaroid--rechts">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-familie-gepaeckwagen-brisbane-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-familie-gepaeckwagen-brisbane-p960.jpg"
        alt="Luggage trolley stacked with olive-green duffel bags in the arrivals hall of Brisbane airport, Joris in profile next to it"
        loading="lazy"
        width="960"
        height="540"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="polaroid__caption">Three lives in duffel bags, Brisbane airport, 28 July, shortly after eight.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Welcome to Australia: after that we stood in traffic for two hours. The kids took it better than we did. Arrived in Byron Bay, we briefly dropped the bags at the Airbnb, and then we did the only right thing: we went straight to the beach. First family afternoon at the Pacific, Linnea sorting finds out of the sand, Joris chasing the outgoing wave.
  </p>
  <p>
    The following evening, the photo was taken that we had been working towards for six months without knowing it: our first family selfie in Australia.
  </p>
  <figure class="polaroid polaroid--links polaroid--gross">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-erstes-familien-selfie-byron-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-erstes-familien-selfie-byron-p960.jpg"
        alt="First family selfie of the four in Australia: Christian, Lucy with Linnea on her arm and Joris at the dune edge of Byron Bay in evening light, beach and surf behind them"
        loading="lazy"
        width="1280"
        height="960"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="polaroid__caption">The four of us, Byron Bay dunes, 29 July 2026.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <em>For context: all of this we lived ourselves. Family arrival: 28 July 2026, around 8 am local time, Brisbane.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-happens-next">Where we stand now</h2>
  <p>
    As of 5 August 2026: since 31 July we have been living in a housesit in the hinterland near Murwillumbah for four weeks. A large, well-kept property, two easy-going dogs, chickens, a wood stove for the winter evenings. The kids can simply walk out the front door and play. For arriving, this house is a stroke of luck: four weeks without moving, four weeks to plan everything else.
  </p>
  <InlineVideo
    src="/assets/videos/au-waesche-housesit-loop.mp4"
    poster="/assets/images/au-waesche-housesit-loop-poster.jpg"
    caption="Housesit everyday life: taking down the washing against a rainforest backdrop."
    lang="en"
    aspectRatio="16 / 9"
  />
  <p>
    As I write this paragraph, Lucy is in the kitchen and the kids are out on the property with their walkie-talkies, reporting in between what they are up to. It is so good to see them move freely and play outside together. Back home we had a lovely flat, but no garden; a property like this is the exception in Germany, here it is almost standard as long as you don't live in the middle of a big city. The vegetable garden this story started with, we still miss. But the kids are growing up right now exactly the way we hoped this country would let them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesterday we were in Murwillumbah, the small town nearby. School classes outside everywhere: bike training, skate lessons, basketball, kids in shorts in the middle of the Australian winter, everyone moving. That is exactly one of the things we find so good here. And the birds have their own fan now: after a walk, Joris sat down and drew all the birds he had seen, from the cockatoo to the brush turkey.
  </p>
  <figure class="beweis">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-joris-vogelbild-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-joris-vogelbild-p960.jpg"
        alt="Joris' coloured pencil drawing of Australian birds in trees with an orange sun, a cockatoo and a brush turkey"
        loading="lazy"
        width="1100"
        height="825"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="beweis__caption">Evidence · Joris' bird inventory of the first week, coloured pencil on A4</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    What is still open, in all honesty:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Visa:</strong> the nomination sits with my future employer, the one part of the process we don't hold in our own hands. After that comes my own 186 application with Johannes.</li>
    <li><strong>Housing:</strong> the search is on. We viewed a lovely cabin in Mullumbimby and didn't get it. An option near my future workplace is in sight, but nothing is fixed. Without a permanent job the housing market here is tough; we knew that beforehand, and it still feels different once you're in it.</li>
    <li><strong>School and childcare:</strong> Joris' first school day is 3 February 2027, which school depends on where we live. Linnea's childcare is still open. Until then Lucy is primarily with the kids and reactivating her architecture contacts from the Byron Bay days in parallel.</li>
    <li><strong>Car:</strong> the Jeep needs repairs before the next rego, estimated 1,000 to 1,500 AUD. We'll tackle that in the coming weeks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For Lucy, the start was the harder one: the first days were too much at once, especially with the kids, and that weighed on her mood. Understandably. Making decisions about the future is hard when nothing that was self-evident at home is self-evident here yet. The beginning is exhausting because you can't do anything in your usual rhythms. And still, in many small moments, we notice what life here could be like. It is good to see that Joris and Linnea feel this much at home, and that all of it is less hard than we expected beforehand. The kids are content because they are here with us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>For context: lived ourselves, as of 5 August 2026. More articles on everyday life will follow once moments have turned into experience.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-anyway">Would we do it again?</h2>
  <p>
    The question behind all questions, in every search statistic on emigrating too: do you regret it? The answer, one week into being four again: <strong>it is too early for a verdict. But right now we have a very good feeling.</strong> It feels right.
  </p>
  <p>
    We'll say what carries that feeling at the moment: we are basically on holiday right now. I still have time off, Joris isn't in school yet, we do something every day. The everyday life that really decides "regret or not" is still ahead. But one point is already no holiday effect: it is winter, and we are outside every day anyway. Nights get cold, days are pleasantly warm as soon as the sun comes out. That alone is almost worth the whole thing for us, because it is exactly the lifestyle we came here for.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other side remains: the distance to our families is hard. Daily video calls make it okay, but hard it stays. And the risk hasn't gone: no permanent visa, the nomination not in our hands, three to four months of financial runway, Lucy can't work full-time yet, I have to work as a chef for the first two years for visa reasons. If it doesn't work out, we fly back. That has been part of the plan from the start, financially and mentally, and that hasn't changed. It is not a recommendation, it is our deliberate bet, with the advantage that we knew the region.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is our path. We share it on YouTube, Instagram and this blog, not to produce travel inspiration, but so families facing the same decision find real numbers and real stories instead of polished influencer versions. If that interests you, read along. We keep writing, now from the other side of the world.
  </p>

